Autumn 1923. At half past nine they found Amos Reed, the lock-keeper, drowned in his own lock. It looks like a fall from the gate in the dark. You are the county constable. The coroner comes at three. By then you need a case, or it goes down as an accident.

Towpath

A cinder path along the canal. The cottage door is west, the lock north, the lock hut south. Water knocks against the lock gate.

Exits: west, north, south.

> w
w

Kitchen

The cottage kitchen, low and warm, with a black iron stove. The door to the towpath is east, the parlour west, the stairs go up.

A pair of town boots dries by the stove. Hester Reed, Amos's daughter, is here.

Exits: east, west, up.

> examine boots
examine boots

Town boots, wet to the ankle, with canal mud in the welts. Marsh said he never left the parlour.

You write it in your notebook: Marsh was out in the wet.

> e
e

Towpath

A cinder path along the canal. The cottage door is west, the lock north, the lock hut south. Somewhere a rope creaks.

Bert Pike, the lock hand, is here.

Exits: west, north, south.

> n
n

Lock Side

The lock gates stand black against the sky, and a gangplank leads east to a barge. Black water fills the chamber to the brim.

Exits: south, east.

> e
e

Barge Cabin

The cabin of the *Kestrel*: a bunk, a stove the size of a hatbox, the smell of coal and wet rope. A tarpaulin covers something in the corner.

Silas Crane, master of the *Kestrel*, is here, with a mug of something.

Exits: west.

> talk to crane
talk to crane

"Constable. I wait for the dawn and the lock, same as every man here."

> 1
"I need to empty the lock. Have you a windlass?"

"Take the spare. Bring it back."

Hester Reed comes in.

> w
w

Lock Side

The lock gates stand black against the sky, and a gangplank leads east to a barge. Black water fills the chamber to the brim.

Exits: south, east.

> open lower paddles
open lower paddles

You fit Crane's windlass and turn. Water roars out under the lower gate.

> wait
wait

Time passes.

Hester Reed comes in.

> wait
wait

Time passes.

The chamber is empty. Wet ladder rungs go down into the dark.

Hester Reed goes south.

> down
down

Lock Bottom

Mud and weed at the bottom of the empty chamber. The walls rise wet on all sides.

The gear housing contains an oilcloth packet.

Exits: up.

> take pages
take pages

Taken.

You write it in your notebook: the true pages.

> up
up

Lock Side

The lock gates stand black against the sky, and a gangplank leads east to a barge. The chamber is empty. Wet ladder rungs go down into the dark.

Exits: south, east, down.

> s
s

Towpath

A cinder path along the canal. The cottage door is west, the lock north, the lock hut south. Water knocks against the lock gate.

Exits: west, north, south.

> s
s

Lock Hut

A brick hut for the lock hands. Oilskins on a nail, a tool chest under the window.

Bert Pike, the lock hand, is here, asleep on a bench. The tool chest contains a coil of rope.

Exits: north.

> search chest
search chest

You find a windlass.

You write it in your notebook: the weapon.

> n
n

Towpath

A cinder path along the canal. The cottage door is west, the lock north, the lock hut south. Somewhere a rope creaks.

Exits: west, north, south.

> w
w

Kitchen

The cottage kitchen, low and warm, with a black iron stove. The door to the towpath is east, the parlour west, the stairs go up.

A pair of town boots stands by the stove, clean. Ivo Marsh, the toll clerk, is here.

Exits: east, west, up.

> accuse marsh
accuse marsh

You lay the pages and your notes on the table, and tell him of the dented windlass in the tool chest. Marsh reads them, and sits down.

Committed for trial

At dawn the county takes Ivo Marsh to town. The pages go with him, in your coat.